From 08216240a3238c103f2c2d5201233fad6715195a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 19:19:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(ui): the proof slider sweeps on its own, and the
 coverage map is the whole territory again
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

THE SLIDER WAS BEING READ AS A PHOTOGRAPH

A before/after parked at fifty percent tells nobody there is a second image.
Most visitors never find the handle, and neither does anything sampling the
page — which is the whole point of building a wipe rather than two images side
by side.

It now sweeps continuously between 14% and 86%, easing at each end and dwelling
there long enough for the eye to land on a still frame before it reverses.
Never to an edge: both floors stay visible at every moment of the cycle.

THREE PROPERTIES THAT MAKE THAT ACCEPTABLE RATHER THAN OBNOXIOUS

It yields completely on the first touch — pointer, key or focus — and returns
only after four seconds of silence. It never moves under a screen reader that
has focus in it, which is why the value assistive technology reports is always
the paused, accurate one. It stops when the figure leaves the viewport. It does
not run at all against prefers-reduced-motion: disabled in the component, not
merely slowed in the stylesheet.

It is driven through refs rather than state. Sixty frames a second through
useState re-renders the subtree sixty times a second for one number; the sweep
writes the CSS custom property and the input's value directly, and React holds
only what a person set. The input had to become uncontrolled for that — a
controlled value fights the sweep on every frame.

THE MAP WAS A FACTUAL ERROR

A static illustration of the Greater Toronto Area, on a page headed "the same
work everywhere", showing about one sixth of everywhere. It was accurate the
month it was drawn and wrong from the day the territory reached Hamilton. An
image cannot be checked by a guard, so it went stale in silence and would have
done it again.

TerritoryMap reads CORRIDORS and MARKETS. A corridor is a drive, so it is drawn
as one: a spine with its municipalities strung along it in the travel order the
registry already stores, and the territory around each spine is the same path
drawn wide and soft. The only hand-placed geometry in the file is twelve pairs
of numbers, at the top, where they can be read.

One corridor holds the light while the rest fall back, passing to the next every
few seconds, dots pulsing in the lit one. That is not decoration: a hundred and
one markets shown at once show nothing. Hover or focus holds a region; leaving
resumes the cycle — the same contract the slider makes. Reduced motion lights
every region at once and moves nothing, which is the honest still frame of the
same fact.

The border is drawn and labelled, because the territory crosses it and a map
that hides that is telling a story about one country.

WHAT A MACHINE GETS

Not the picture. A role and a full label on the SVG, and underneath it every
corridor and every municipality as ordinary text in a real list. Nothing about
this territory is available only to something that can rasterise an SVG.

THE TWO RETIRED ILLUSTRATIONS

map-service-areas-gta and its variant are deleted — slot, manifest entry,
generated import and webp. verify:images caught them as orphans the moment the
page stopped drawing them, which is the guard doing exactly its job.

SIX NEW TESTS, TWO NEW GUARD CHECKS

The map is tested against the registry rather than against a screenshot: every
corridor has a spine, no spine names a corridor that does not exist, every
municipality lands inside the frame with room for its halo, the jitter is
deterministic (a random one would break hydration), and no municipality name is
hardcoded anywhere in the component. verify:sliders now fails if the sweep, the
hand-over, the resume delay, the off-screen stop, the reduced-motion opt-out or
the uncontrolled input is removed. Regression run and observed.

66 guards green, 323 tests, tsc at its 115-error baseline.

